WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.860
- The Gotthard Base Tunnel

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.740
is the longest and deepest
rail tunnel in the world,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.721
57 kilometers through the Alps.

00:00:06.721 --> 00:00:08.790
Literally, through.
It's called a "base tunnel"

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.410
because it's at the base of the mountain.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.920
At its deepest point, there are more than
two kilometers of rock above.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.580
It's so far down into the ground
that the earth in there is hot.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.700
Without ventilation, the air temperature
will be more than 40°.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.900
Around the turn of the century,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.170
there were two devastating fires
in road tunnels under those mountains,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.610
and the authorities here in Switzerland

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.520
want to make sure that nothing like that
can ever happen again.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.080
How they do that is fascinating.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.400
So here's what's going to happen.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.070
We're going to see the control center
that watches over the tunnel,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.470
and also visit one of
the intervention centers,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.270
with the firefighters
and first responders

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.760
that'll step in if the
worst were to happen.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.310
And then I want to show you
what I reckon is the most

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.840
important bit of safety equipment.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.250
It isn't anywhere near the tunnel,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.440
and most people will
probably never notice it.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.085
But before that, we start
at the control center,

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:53.310
just over there.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.301
- In the Gotthard Base Tunnel, there are 

00:00:55.301 --> 00:01:00.420
six freight trains and two passenger trains,
per hour, per direction.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.510
This means that at the same
time, there are several trains

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.030
inside the tunnel, in
both of the two tubes.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.090
The Gotthard Base Tunnel is
divided into three thirds.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.940
If there is a disturbance
in one of these thirds,

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.840
the train can be diverted
at one of the two changement points,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.460
into the other tube,
to bypass the disturbed section.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.010
There are different sections
to control the traffic.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.170
The signal box controls the track.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.620
The movement authority is
given by the signal box,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.330
and by the traffic controller.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.430
If there is a disturbance on one
of the systems in the tunnel, 

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.860
then we have different
possibilities.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.343
First of all, we get noticed by an alarm
that there is a problem.

00:01:38.343 --> 00:01:42.990
They first of all try to
fix the problem remotely.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.700
If this is not successful,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.690
they can send people on
site to try to fix it.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.210
- This tunnel is actually a bit of a
tourist attraction in itself.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.370
You can get driven in,
down the service tunnel,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:58.530
on a guided tour, and end up at this
reinforced, windproof, visitor's window

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.890
with 8cm-thick glass,
and watch the trains go by

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.123
at anything up to 230km/h.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.282
That was terrifying!

00:02:13.346 --> 00:02:15.780
- If a train has a technical
problem, first of all

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.440
the train driver tries to
reboot the train system.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.010
If it's not successful,
we have the firefighting train,

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:27.330
which goes into the tunnel
to tow out the broken train.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.270
In case of an incident in
the tunnel, let's say a fire,

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.970
we have two intervention
centers, North and South.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.700
"Intervention center" means
firefighting stations.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.190
They get an alarm, and
within five minutes,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.860
usually both of the firefighting trains

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.440
go into the tunnel
from north and from south

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.380
at the same time to fight the fire.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.620
They have the obligation to
be on any point in the tunnel

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.120
within 45 minutes.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.380
Firefighting train is a convoy
of firefighting trucks on rail.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:00.360
They bring water in, foam liquid.
They bring special equipment, tools,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.690
to handle any possible
situation inside the tunnel.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.980
At the two emergency stops,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.990
there is a possibility
to refill their water.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.750
If people have to be
evacuated in the tunnel,

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.480
they do not walk out of the tunnel
because it's much too far.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.980
The train brings them to one
of two evacuation stations,

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.060
where the passengers can go
into the evacuation tube.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.790
This evacuation tube is under pressure,
so fresh air is streaming in.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.610
They are safe because
they have enough oxygen,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.590
and smoke cannot enter.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.770
Then they go to the
next evacuation section,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.230
where the evacuation train
is waiting for them.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.930
We have to be able to
evacuate the entire tunnel

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.280
within 90 minutes.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.170
- As I went round today,
one of the team said

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.050
that if there ever is an
evacuation or a fire in the tunnel,

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.570
several other things
had to go wrong first.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.620
Yes, you can never protect

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.250
against every possible weird
circumstance, but you can try.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.350
Which brings me here,

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:56.889
to what's basically the
middle of nowhere in Switzerland,

00:03:56.889 --> 00:03:59.820
about 15km away from the
south entrance to the tunnel,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.250
and to this gantry over the tracks.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.700
15km sounds like a lot, but at the
speed Swiss trains can travel,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.830
that could be about four or five minutes.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.240
And while it doesn't look like much,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.560
this gantry scans every train, at speed,
before it even gets near the tunnel.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.360
There are sensors on the
track around here too,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.180
and if the train has anything unbalanced, any hot spots that look out of place,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.410
anything poking out,
then it's not going into the tunnel.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.310
- We have laser systems which measure
the profile of a train.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.790
Maybe the load of a train has moved,

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:34.050
and doesn't respect any more
the necessary profile to enter a tunnel.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:37.770
We have heat detection,
where we can detect

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.770
overheated axles or
overheated brakes.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:44.460
All these controls, they happen
at usual commercial speed

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.090
of the trains, in different
points, all over Switzerland,

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:52.950
and we get about 200 alarms per day
from these measuring stations.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.580
If we do not confirm an
alarm within 30 seconds,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.350
the train is stopped automatically.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:02.250
It's not an emergency stop, it's...
but it's quite... you feel it!

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.620
If a train seems to be wrong,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.770
it gets to a side track
where it has to wait.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.960
If the inspection says,
this train is okay,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.670
then it can continue its journey,

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:15.090
if not, the owner of the train
has to resolve the problem

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.460
before the train can continue its journey.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.390
- Yes, there are lots of systems in place
for if "the worst happens",

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.520
but prevention is better than cure,

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.280
and for me, this gantry
is the most important bit

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.450
of safety equipment here,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.060
because the best solution
is to stop problems

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.533
before they even start.

